Subject: Re: Hotpath cutover: SDK migration, circuit breaker & client-side rate fencing

Elena — quick answers and an implementation plan from the infra/SDK side.

Short answers: 
- Signal for local short-circuit: SDK will return a 499-style client-fence code and add header X-Redwood-Client-Fence: true. This separates local fencing in your telemetry.
- Half-open probe cadence: 1 probe every 10s with capped concurrency of 2 probes per process. If probe succeeds for 3 consecutive probes, close the circuit.

Implementation plan (deliverables & owners):
- 2026-09-13: SDK RC (python + go + node) with token-bucket defaults: 400 tokens per process, refill 100 tokens/s (Irene).
- 2026-09-14: Integration test with Finovate staging (Vanessa + Finovate ops) — run spike to 600 qps for 60s to validate short-circuit behavior and telemetry.
- 2026-09-15: Docs + sample snippets published to Drive and included in release notes (Kimberly).
- 2026-09-18 02:00-04:00 UTC: Cutover window — SDK pinned to RC on hotpath clients; Redwood will monitor and stand by (Irene, Vanessa).

Sample Go snippet (rate fence + jittered backoff):
```go
// pseudo Go client: token bucket + backoff
func sendWithFence(req *Request) (*Response, error) {
  if !tokenBucket.Allow() { // local short-circuit
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("client-fence:429")
  }
  for attempt := 0; attempt < 4; attempt++ {
    resp, err := client.Post(req)
    if err == nil && resp.StatusCode == 200 { return resp, nil }
    if resp != nil && (resp.StatusCode == 429 || resp.StatusCode == 503) {
      // exponential backoff with full jitter
      base := 200 * time.Millisecond
      sleep := time.Duration(float64(base) * math.Pow(2, float64(attempt)))
      sleep = time.Duration(rand.Float64() * float64(sleep))
      time.Sleep(sleep)
      continue
    }
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("unexpected: %v", err)
  }
  return nil, fmt.Errorf("max retries")
}
```

Notes on per-process vs per-container token-bucket: since Finovate uses short-lived containers, we recommend per-process defaults (token bucket in each process) and an optional cross-process limiter (sidecar) if you want strict global quotas. For the cutover, per-process will be fine; the SDK RC includes an env var to tune tokens/refill.

